#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1020.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125655} :activity/id #uuid "feaa62a5-6a44-51be-9efd-89d2c7e4938d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048450 :topic/id #uuid "a059bba1-760f-57e5-af3d-31e6c7347897" :topic/math-academy-id 1947} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125655 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125892 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to find the **global maximum** and **global minimum** of a function $f(x,y).$ Recall that the global maximum and minimum of a function are collectively known as **global extrema.** \n\nThe procedure we will describe for finding the global extrema is based on a general principle called the **extreme-value theorem,** which states the following:\n\n*Every function that is continuous on a closed, bounded region $D$ has global extrema (i.e., both a global maximum and a global minimum) on $D$*.\n\nLet's consider the function $f(x,y) =  e^{x^2- y^2}$ on the closed  region $D=\\{(x,y)\\in \\mathbb R^2: \\,  x^2 +y^2  \\leq 1\\}.$ How do we find the global extrema?\n\nTo find the global extrema of a differentiable function on a closed, bounded region $D,$ we follow these steps:\n\n**Step 1**:  Determine the critical points of $f$ in the interior of $D.$\n\n![](images/50/5057a4a96daa4819a70e733519c89802c28bde184183db2b4efbe69d3db36e56.png)\n\nTo determine the critical points, we solve $\\boldsymbol f' = \\mathbf 0.$ Computing the derivative gives\n\n![](images/b4/b48de1dcd3a240a1f1904dd290ef0665fc603c4cc5f99915915c6a12f79d74d9.svg)\n\nThe derivative exists at each point inside $D,$ and it equals zero when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}2x{e}^{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}=0 \\\\ -2y{e}^{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x=0 \\\\ y=0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis gives us a single point $(0,0).$ Since the point $(0,0)$ is in the interior of $D,$ we include it in the list of candidates for extrema.\n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of ${D}$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the boundary points that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize the bounding circle $x^2 + y^2 =1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\langle \\cos{t}, \\sin{t} \\rangle, \\qquad t \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & ={e}^{{cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t \\in (0,2\\pi),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ ({e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)})^{′} & =0 \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t){e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)} & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation has three solutions, namely \n\n$$\nt =\\dfrac\\pi 2, \\: \\pi, \\: \\dfrac{3\\pi}2 \\in (0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nWe also have to check the endpoint of the interval, namely, $t=0.$ Therefore, the only values of $t$ that can give rise to extreme values are \n\n$$\nt=0, \\: \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\:  \\pi, \\: \\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ for each value of $t$, we obtain the following candidate points:\n\n$$\n(1,0), \\: (0,1), \\: (-1,0), \\: (0,-1)\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values.\n\nEvaluating $f$ at the five points we've found gives the following table of values:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(1,0)$ | $(0,1)$ | $(-1,0)$ | $(0,-1)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $1$ | $e$ | $1/e$ | $e$ | $1/e$ |\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $\\dfrac1e$ at the points $(0,1), \\, (0,-1),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $e$ at the points $(1,0), \\, (-1,0).$" :tutorial/id #uuid "b8b379fb-f7e4-5cdb-b25a-69a6c2ae9cf2" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7879 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "85def1da-b072-51a0-ae08-591a0d352331" :step/math-academy-id 45426 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125656}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125656 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124656 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124219 :question/id #uuid "14c9cf83-8dce-598b-b9cb-ec22966d8fac" :question/math-academy-id "e-12587"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e38cefde-a663-56de-a81d-dcea6fed89b3" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048162} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048621}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125033} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125063} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284576} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354505} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404350} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404371} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446480} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446484} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446490} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484220} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484239}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Global Maxima and Minima Given the Critical Points Inside the Function's Domain"} :step/id #uuid "ac225680-0726-58b8-b03b-f62ad289bb51" :step/math-academy-id 45427 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125657}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125657 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124657 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124220 :question/id #uuid "84d1abb1-8b2b-5180-8924-825386924270" :question/math-academy-id "e-12588"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "608bbe95-edf0-54a6-bd05-5578ba073319" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048385} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049672} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050722}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125035} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125064} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351509} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354504} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363541} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401579} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404364} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446479} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446482} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446486} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446487} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446489}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Global Maxima and Minima of a Function Whose Domain Is a Smooth Curve"} :step/id #uuid "7c1f6a3a-a0f9-5d34-85e4-3cbe4e6bca0e" :step/math-academy-id 45430 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125658}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125658 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125893 :tutorial/content "Consider a function $f(\\mathbf{x})$ and a closed and bounded region $D$ enclosed by several curves, like the one shown below.\n\n![](images/04/042e4b736bdaa93033af34cc4ff738d1976a6595a448c8b697e717a74d19b67f.png)\n\nIn such cases, we need to search for extrema candidates among the following three categories:\n\nI. The critical points of $f$ that belong to the interior of $D.$\n\nII. The critical points of the function $f(\\mathbf{r}),$ where $\\mathbf{r}$ traverses each of the given parts of the boundary. In the example above, we would have to parametrize each of the $\\color{blue}4$ parts of the boundary separately.\n\nIII. The corner points of the boundary, i.e., the points where the different parts of the boundary meet. In the example above, we'd have to consider $\\color{red}4$ such points." :tutorial/id #uuid "e1ab24b0-a5c2-5d72-bc3e-614864010365" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7880 :tutorial/title "Determining the Candidates for Extrema on a Piecewise Smooth Boundary"} :step/id #uuid "8d1553dd-1fe4-5a34-9376-7547f1b2b500" :step/math-academy-id 45433 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125659}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125659 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124658 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124221 :question/id #uuid "138b3374-23fe-5705-87ed-e9e27a19e186" :question/math-academy-id "e-12589"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "82691436-7076-5c46-bb45-462ea9218db8" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049508}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125037} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125065} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284579} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351513} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363548} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373325} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399180} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404252} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404357} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446481} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446483} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446488}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Global Maxima and Minima of a Function Whose Domain Is Piecewise Smooth"} :step/id #uuid "ccc80dce-cc5f-5576-95e2-9236c9281336" :step/math-academy-id 45434}] :activity/title "Calculating Global Extrema of Multivariable Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124737}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484306} :activity/id #uuid "014bb5c2-0eb1-526d-946d-25c9eb45386c" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048450 :topic/id #uuid "a059bba1-760f-57e5-af3d-31e6c7347897" :topic/math-academy-id 1947} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484299 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404350 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ead01c60-0b8e-5265-95fa-6b4cdaec742d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-1$ and ${f}_{max}=7$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404352} {:answer/id #uuid "db0835b3-9555-5f10-b783-ce2279912fb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-3$ and ${f}_{max}=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404353} {:answer/id #uuid "c48ddddc-9d40-5c48-8b18-1935496f2905" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-7$ and ${f}_{max}=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404354} {:answer/id #uuid "23d9111e-7ce1-561b-9f4f-29ebc2c46ba5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-7$ and ${f}_{max}=7$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404355} {:answer/id #uuid "31a2fcdf-48af-528a-bc62-4f070d949ae8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-3$ and ${f}_{max}=7$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404356}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404354} :answer-field/id #uuid "c1c6b9bc-2cad-5aef-8fbc-0df5f811dfbb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404351}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "32f5ebff-a024-5f63-b943-142f3a504252" :question/math-academy-id "q-86709" :question/problem "Find the global extrema of the function $f(x,y)=2xy+6x-3$ on the closed region $D$ enclosed by $y=-{x}^{2}$ and $y=-4.$ You are given that $f$ has a critical point at $(0,-3),$ and on the boundary of the region the points that could give rise to extreme values are $(-2,-4),$ $(-1,-1),$ $(1,-1),$ and $(2,-4).$" :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points inside the region $D.$ \n\nTo determine the critical points, let's first sketch the region:\n\n![](images/96/96096b5f8944785f1af1bbc4fd4942e081b4b3999887c0fa8d5d76008af454d5.png)\n\nSince the point $(0,-3)$ is in the interior of $D,$ we include it in the list of candidates for extrema.\n\n**Step 2.** Find the points on the boundary that could give rise to extreme values.\n\nIn this case, we are *given* the points on the boundary of our region that could potentially be global extrema for $f.$ These are\n\n$$\n(-2,-4),\\,(-1,-1),\\,(1,-1),\\,(2,-4).\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,-3) & =2(0)(-3)+6(0)-3 \\\\  & =-3 \\\\ f(-2,-4) & =2(-2)(-4)+6(-2)-3 \\\\  & =1 \\\\ f(-1,-1) & =2(-1)(-1)+6(-1)-3 \\\\  & =-7 \\\\ f(1,-1) & =2(1)(-1)+6(1)-3 \\\\  & =1 \\\\ f(2,-4) & =2(2)(-4)+6(2)-3 \\\\  & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-7$ at the points $(-1,-1)$ and $(2,-4),$\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $1$ at the points $(-2,-4)$ and $(1,-1).$"} :step/id #uuid "0072a223-d977-5b15-a887-a851fc97c6d7" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484346}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484306 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484220 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a98d925-ab88-52f8-884f-7e6b8120f7cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-2$ and ${f}_{max}=16$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483675} {:answer/id #uuid "3d039668-18b1-5d4a-9869-1e01eaef1bf8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=0$ and ${f}_{max}=4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483679} {:answer/id #uuid "589e8125-b8d8-5115-be4b-3627bde6a5ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=1$ and ${f}_{max}=4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483729} {:answer/id #uuid "edc7a1cd-95f8-56b1-b089-7c0beaf8c25d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-2$ and ${f}_{max}=0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484038} {:answer/id #uuid "f3c7bece-f4b8-5686-919a-a5cb331f67ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=0$ and ${f}_{max}=16$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484057}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484057} :answer-field/id #uuid "750f6f9e-b6e0-5035-8e03-025f023dadec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484136}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "50860c27-301d-5231-9268-fbee285d7c09" :question/math-academy-id "q-37146" :question/problem "Find the global extrema of the function $f(x,y)=(x-1)^{2}+{y}^{2}$ on the closed region  $D=\\{(x,y):{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 9\\}.$ You are given that $f$ has a critical point at  $(1,0)$ and on the boundary of the region the points that could give rise to extreme values are  $(-3,0)$, and $(3,0).$" :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$ \n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points inside the region $D.$ \n\nTo determine the critical points, let's first sketch the region:\n\n![](images/d7/d7864a36585a145e009846b23142372caeff3a68f17c9d1f7d1cf17e642f9543.png)\n\nSince the critical point $(1,0)$ lies inside $D,$ we include that point in the list of candidates for the extrema. \n\n**Step 2.** Find the points on the boundary that could give rise to extreme values.\n\nIn this case, we are *given* the points on the boundary of our region that could potentially be global extrema for $f.$ These are\n\n$$\n(3,0),\\,(-3,0).\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,0) & =(1-1)^{2}+(0)^{2} \\\\  & =0 \\\\ f(3,0) & =(2)^{2}+(0)^{2} \\\\  & =4 \\\\ f(-3,0) & =(-4)^{2}+(0)^{2} \\\\  & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $0$ at the point $(1,0),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $16$ at the point $(-3,0).$"} :step/id #uuid "13d0691c-2114-546b-9bbe-2348cd351497" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484315}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484315 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125035 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "51e8f2b2-e881-54be-8bfe-5a90a63da983" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=0$ and ${f}_{max}=25$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321793} {:answer/id #uuid "58776b4d-dfad-5418-8461-511cc2520627" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-5$ and ${f}_{max}=5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321843} {:answer/id #uuid "6a96826c-9c27-5fd9-82e2-7b681ef6be75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-1$ and ${f}_{max}=0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321978} {:answer/id #uuid "94d8e7cb-be9a-518f-a277-ee731c13382d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-1$ and ${f}_{max}=5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322333} {:answer/id #uuid "f6f68fc9-b908-5124-a8a9-322a05564d15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=1$ and ${f}_{max}=25$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323085}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321793} :answer-field/id #uuid "eedde66f-83fc-5633-be9a-580fbbeb170c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323640}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "db6b4d4c-efe8-5d52-b8df-1c7481ef870b" :question/math-academy-id "q-37155" :question/problem "Find the global extrema of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4x+4$ on the closed region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 9\\}.$ You are given that the function has a critical point at $(2,0)\\in D.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ that lie inside the region $D.$ \n\nWe have been given that $f$ has a critical point at \n\n$\\,$ $(2,0)$ \n\nwhich belongs to the interior of the region $D.$ So, we include this point in the list of candidates for the extrema. \n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of $D$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the boundary points that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize the bounding circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9.$ This gives\n\n$$\nr=⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}-4(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)+4 \\\\  & =9{cos}^{2}⁡t+9{sin}^{2}⁡t-12\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+4 \\\\  & =13-12\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t\\in (0,2\\pi ),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ (13-12\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{′} & =0 \\\\ 12\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation gives one solution $t=\\pi \\in (0,2\\pi ).$\nWe also have to check the endpoint of the interval $t=0.$ Therefore, the only values of $t$ that can possibly give rise to extreme values are $t=0,\\,\\pi .$ Evaluating the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ on the boundary, we obtain two candidate points:\n\n$$\n(3,0),\\,(-3,0)\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values.\n\nLet's compare the values of $f(x,y)$ evaluated at each of the points mentioned above. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2,0) & =(2)^{2}+(0)^{2}-4(2)+4 \\\\  & =4+0-8+4 \\\\  & =0 \\\\ f(3,0) & =(3)^{2}+(0)^{2}-4(3)+4 \\\\  & =9+0-12+4 \\\\  & =1 \\\\ f(-3,0) & =(-3)^{2}+(0)^{2}-4(-3)+4 \\\\  & =9+0+12+4 \\\\  & =25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $0$ at the point $(2,0),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $25$ at the point $(-3,0).$"} :step/id #uuid "37a5ea02-37b7-5758-8571-8626bf94da23" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484340}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484340 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484239 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37223220-8ce2-5aaf-b13b-e51831642442" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483667} {:answer/id #uuid "4141a628-787e-59cd-9b3b-4d9ed6c8303b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483690} {:answer/id #uuid "42c9840a-d25c-56c5-a13d-22ba7d99829d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483691} {:answer/id #uuid "91ce1e4d-9d5f-507c-b1a8-339a62262fd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483870} {:answer/id #uuid "fde67504-6547-54d3-949e-2a67417d8d96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484080}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483691} :answer-field/id #uuid "585700db-4a4c-5c97-98d7-30b257ff65a1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484125}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "798ecbfd-5ca4-5e85-b538-375fc7b94df7" :question/math-academy-id "q-83400" :question/problem "Find the global maximum of the function $f(x,y)=2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4x-2y$ on the closed triangular region  $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 2-x\\}.$ You're given that $f$ has a critical point at $(1,1)$ and on the boundary of the triangle the points that could give rise to extreme values are the vertices." :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ that lie inside the region $D.$ \n\nTo determine the critical points, let's first sketch the region:\n\n![](images/30/30a739bfccac0a1ccc405b7ce1fd34afd46b5a2f436c852cef0bb38a13ec1e64.png)\n\n \n\nSince the point $(1,1)$ does not belong to the interior of $D$, we do not yet include it in the list of candidates for extrema (it lies on the boundary of $D,$ so we will consider it in the next step).\n\n**Step 2.** Find the points on the boundary that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nIn this case, we are *given* the points on the boundary of our region that could potentially be global extrema for $f.$ These are\n\n$$\n(0,2),\\,(2,0),\\,(0,0),\\,(1,1).\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,2) & =2(0)^{2}+(2)^{2}-4(0)-2(2) \\\\  & =4-4 \\\\  & =0 \\\\ f(2,0) & =2(2)^{2}+(0)^{2}-4(2)-2(0) \\\\  & =8-8 \\\\  & =0 \\\\ f(0,0) & =2(0)^{2}+(0)^{2}-4(0)-2(0) \\\\  & =0 \\\\ f(1,1) & =2(1)^{2}+(1)^{2}-4(1)-2(1) \\\\  & =2+1-4-2 \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-3$ at the point $(1,1),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $0$ at the points $(0,0),(0,2),$ and $(2,0).$"} :step/id #uuid "af6a13b7-97bf-5a56-b47d-072d6ba6485e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484299}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484346 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354505 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "148c3c70-3db7-55f7-8111-88c0343f9bd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt[4]{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351746} {:answer/id #uuid "2b5b8adf-77e5-5a78-94b3-8706a2ac286c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{e}^{-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351960} {:answer/id #uuid "9eacec04-ca0d-50a6-98e3-da77f9822ab8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352924} {:answer/id #uuid "cf33c688-22a9-5f87-ab62-f656a7ea1b5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353362} {:answer/id #uuid "e1bb70c0-87ec-593e-850c-1115f0e6beea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353526}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351746} :answer-field/id #uuid "8bd8154f-1898-5372-9fda-b77df68be7d1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354068}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9e5511a5-af5b-54bb-a9ab-113418cf34fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-86725" :question/problem "Find the global maximum of the function $f(x,y)=2{e}^{xy-x}$ on the closed triangular region  $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 2-x\\}.$ You're given that $f$ has a critical point at $(0,1)$ and on the boundary of the triangle the points that could give rise to extreme values are the vertices and the point $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ that lie inside the region $D.$ \n\nTo determine the critical points, let's first sketch the region:\n\n![](images/42/42b9bcda74e29cf7012fd9576ee7bcea92bfc3f58fab4c43c854892afe3acc44.png)\n\n \n\nSince the point $(0,1)$ does not belong to the interior of $D$, we do not yet include it in the list of candidates for extrema (it lies on the boundary of $D,$ so we will consider it in the next step).\n\n**Step 2.** Find the points on the boundary that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nIn this case, we are *given* the points on the boundary of our region that could potentially be global extrema for $f.$ These are\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(2,0),\\,(0,1),\\,(0,2),\\,(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2})\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,0) & =2{e}^{(0)(0)-(0)} \\\\  & =2 \\\\ f(2,0) & =2{e}^{(2)(0)-(2)} \\\\  & =2{e}^{-2} \\\\ f(0,1) & =2{e}^{(0)(1)-(0)} \\\\  & =2 \\\\ f(0,2) & =2{e}^{(0)(2)-(0)} \\\\  & =2 \\\\ f(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2}) & =2{e}^{(1/2)(3/2)-(1/2)} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt[4]{e}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $2{e}^{-2}$ at the point $(2,0),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $2\\sqrt[4]{e}$ at the point $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2}).$"} :step/id #uuid "bcac2916-b3e2-509d-93c5-20d8fd6bdd8a"}] :activity/title "Calculating Global Extrema of Multivariable Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483555}]}
